{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And they shall eat the flesh.</b> Therefore the people are accustomed to make on the night of Pesach three matzot, in remembrance of the three se'ahs that it says: ״three se'ahs [of flour], and the Pesach was prepared.״ And some say: in remembrance of the three patriarchs. And breaking it [the matzah] is in remembrance of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds and the Jordan, which split on Pesach. And the blessing over the broken piece is for the eating of matzah, as is stated in Pesaḥim: what is the practice of a poor person? — a broken piece and two dips, corresponding to the two dips that a homeborn and a purchased slave dipped. Another explanation: corresponding to the two splashes of blood, one on the lintel and one on the doorposts. Another explanation: corresponding to the blood of circumcision and the blood of Pesach, as it is said: ״מתבוססת בדמיך״. And in Pesaḥim it is proven that they are for recognition [le-hakra] by the children, who were accustomed to eat vegetables after eating, for cleaning [the teeth]. And now they eat before the meal. They will ask: what is different? And another distinctive thing [has further], for when they see on other festivals that they drink only one cup beforehand, and now they pour a second cup — then they will ask. And so it concludes there, and here the son asks. And ḥaroset is in remembrance of the clay, and they put apples in it, in remembrance of the apple, as it is written above: ״לעיל תחת התפוח עוררתיך״, and spices that resemble straw, in remembrance of the straw, and nuts and almonds, which whiten the ḥaroset, in remembrance of the clay that is made from lime, which is white. And likewise it is brought in Pesaḥim: spices in remembrance of straw, ḥaroset in remembrance of clay, two cooked dishes in remembrance of Moses and Aaron. Another explanation: fish corresponding to Leviathan; egg corresponding to the ziz of Shaddai. And in Pesaḥim it concludes: one in remembrance of Pesach and one in remembrance of the festival offering [ḥagiga]. Four cups corresponding to the four expressions of redemption: ״והוצאתי״, ״והצלתי״, ״וגאלתי״, ״ולקחתי״. And a fifth cup — that is, for one who needs [to drink it as] his drink — it corresponds to ״והבאתי״, which is also a redemption, as people say: one who emancipated his slave and handed over all his property into his hand — if he does not bring him into his house, what does it benefit him? That is: a slave whose master emancipated him and handed his goblet into his hand — if he does not bring him into his dwelling, what good is all the kindness to him? So too here, if the Holy One, blessed be He, did not bring them to the land of Israel, what would the exodus from Egypt have availed them? Another explanation: four cups corresponding to the four deliverances of HaShem: ״ה' מנת חלקי וכוסי״, ״כוסי רויה״, ״כוס ישועות אשא״ — it does not say 'deliverance' [singular] but 'deliverances' [plural]: one for the days of the Messiah and one for the world to come. Another explanation: corresponding to the four cups that the chief butler told Joseph. Another explanation: corresponding to the four cups of retribution that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to make the nations drink, as it is said: ״קח את כוס החמה״, and it is said: ״כי כוס ביד ה'״. And likewise ״כוס חמה ביד ה'״, which is written concerning Babylon. And it is said: ״ורוח זלעפות מנת כוסם״."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The phrase ״מהרי שלש סאים ופסח היה״ is rendered as a scriptural-style citation (likely referencing Genesis 18:6 language combined with a Pesach tradition); left untranslated as a quotation per divine-name and quotation rules. The word תפוח in the Song citation may refer to the citrus/etrog or apple; rendered as 'apple' following convention."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:10.881266705Z", :translated-from :he, 